WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.922
XSLT is not just formatting.

2
00:00:01.922 --> 00:00:14.211
A stylesheet describes transformation logic that can select data, combine values, produce new documents, and sometimes interact with extension behavior depending on configuration.

3
00:00:14.211 --> 00:00:22.861
For security review, treat stylesheets as code-like input because they influence what the application reads and what it emits.

4
00:00:22.861 --> 00:00:28.490
Do not accept and execute untrusted stylesheets in ordinary application workflows.

5
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:38.101
If a transformation is needed, keep the stylesheet in an application-controlled location, version it, review it, and deploy it deliberately.

6
00:00:38.101 --> 00:00:47.369
A partner-supplied or user-supplied stylesheet should be treated as a special design requirement, not as a default convenience feature.

7
00:00:47.369 --> 00:00:55.332
Review scripting, extension objects, external document access, imports, includes, parameters, and resolver behavior.

8
00:00:55.332 --> 00:01:02.472
These features can be useful in controlled environments, but they expand what the transformation can do.

9
00:01:02.472 --> 00:01:08.170
Keep them disabled unless there is a documented and reviewed reason to enable them.

10
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.122
Transformation output needs its own review.

11
00:01:11.122 --> 00:01:20.047
A workflow may transform XML into HTML, JavaScript-bearing pages, CSV files, office documents, reports, emails, or downstream XML.

12
00:01:20.047 --> 00:01:29.109
Each target format has different output encoding, formula handling, content-type, data exposure, and viewer behavior considerations.

13
00:01:29.109 --> 00:01:31.717
Errors and diagnostics should be safe.

14
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:40.299
Transformation failures can reveal local file paths, internal resource names, sensitive XML values, or configuration details.

15
00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:51.214
Production behavior should give users a clear failure outcome without dumping internals, while logs should retain safe operational context for troubleshooting.

16
00:01:51.214 --> 00:02:03.709
A safe XSLT baseline is straightforward: use trusted stylesheets, restrict optional features, keep resource access controlled, review output format risks, and document any exception.

17
00:02:03.709 --> 00:02:10.436
That turns transformations from a hidden behavior into an explicit part of the application design.
